\description{
Baselga's partition of the Sorensen and Jaccard dissimilarities between
two samples into \strong{turnover} (features replaced by others) and
\strong{nestedness-resultant} dissimilarity (one sample holding a subset of
the other's features).
}
\details{
With \eqn{a} features in both samples, and \eqn{b} and \eqn{c} only in
the first and only in the second (Baselga 2010, 2012):\tabular{lll}{
   Function \tab Component \tab Formula \cr
   \code{sorensen()} \tab Sorensen dissimilarity (total) \tab \eqn{\frac{b + c}{2a + b + c}} \cr
   \code{sorensen_turnover()} \tab Simpson dissimilarity, \eqn{\beta_{sim}} \tab \eqn{\frac{\min(b, c)}{a + \min(b, c)}} \cr
   \code{sorensen_nestedness()} \tab nestedness-resultant, \eqn{\beta_{sne}} \tab \eqn{\beta_{sor} - \beta_{sim}} \cr
   \code{jaccard()} \tab Jaccard dissimilarity (total) \tab \eqn{\frac{b + c}{a + b + c}} \cr
   \code{jaccard_turnover()} \tab \eqn{\beta_{jtu}} \tab \eqn{\frac{2\min(b, c)}{a + 2\min(b, c)}} \cr
   \code{jaccard_nestedness()} \tab \eqn{\beta_{jne}} \tab \eqn{\beta_{jac} - \beta_{jtu}} \cr
}


Turnover is 0 when one sample's features are a subset of the other's,
and 1 when the samples share none; nestedness is 0 when they have the
same number of features. Turnover and nestedness add up to the total,
\code{\link[=sorensen]{sorensen()}} or \code{\link[=jaccard]{jaccard()}}.

When a sample has no features, turnover and nestedness are undefined
(\code{NaN}), as in abdiv and betapart, although the total is 1 (or \code{NaN}
when both samples are empty).

Neither component is a metric: a sample nested in two others has
turnover 0 to both, while they can have turnover 1 to each other.

The partition is debated: Podani & Schmera (2011) and Carvalho et al.
(2012) split the same totals differently, into replacement and richness
difference (adespatial's \code{beta.div.comp()}). See Baselga & Leprieur
(2015) for a comparison.

\strong{Same names in other packages:} abdiv's functions are named
\code{sorenson_turnover()} and \code{sorenson_nestedness()} (with an "o"), and
\code{jaccard_turnover()} and \code{jaccard_nestedness()}, taking two vectors; they
give the same values. betapart's \code{beta.pair()} returns all three
matrices of a family, as \code{\link[=beta_partition]{beta_partition()}} does.
}
